        content: `WHS LEGISLATION, DUTY OF CARE & RISK MANAGEMENT (CPCWHS1001)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: THE AUSTRALIAN WHS LEGISLATIVE FRAMEWORK
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The Work Health and Safety (WHS) Act 2011 is the cornerstone of workplace safety law across most of Australia. It was developed through a national harmonisation process and adopted by the Commonwealth, New South Wales, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania, the Australian Capital Territory, and the Northern Territory. Western Australia and Victoria maintain their own separate but substantially equivalent legislation — the Work Health and Safety Act 2020 (WA) and the Occupational Health and Safety Act 2004 (VIC).

The purpose of the WHS Act is clearly stated: to secure the health, safety, and welfare of workers and workplaces by eliminating, or where that is not reasonably practicable, minimising risks arising from work. This is not merely aspirational — it creates enforceable legal obligations on multiple parties.

Supporting the Act are the Work Health and Safety Regulations 2017, which provide detailed, prescriptive requirements for specific hazards and industries, including excavation, confined spaces, electrical safety, and high-risk construction work. Alongside these sit Codes of Practice — practical guides developed by Safe Work Australia that are admissible as evidence in legal proceedings. Following a Code of Practice is one way to demonstrate compliance with the Act, but it is not the only way — as long as an equivalent or better level of safety is achieved.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: WHO HAS DUTIES UNDER THE WHS ACT?
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The WHS Act recognises that workplace safety is a shared responsibility. It defines specific duty holders and their obligations:

THE PCBU (Person Conducting a Business or Undertaking)
The PCBU is the primary duty holder — typically the company or employer. The PCBU must ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable, the health and safety of workers while at work and others who may be affected by its work. This duty extends to:

→ Providing and maintaining a work environment that is without risks to health and safety. This means safe buildings, walkways, work platforms, lighting, ventilation, and emergency exits — not just at the start of a project, but throughout its entire life.

→ Providing and maintaining safe plant and structures. Every crane, scaffold system, concrete saw, extension lead, and scaffolding plank must be fit for purpose, regularly inspected, and maintained. A faulty circular saw, a scaffold plank with a crack, or a crane with a worn hook is a failure of this duty.

→ Providing and maintaining safe systems of work. This includes written procedures (such as Safe Work Method Statements), proper inductions, toolbox talks, permit systems, and task planning. A system of work is the way a task is organised and performed — and it must be safe even under foreseeable circumstances such as fatigue, time pressure, and environmental changes.

→ Providing adequate welfare facilities. Toilets, hand-washing facilities, clean drinking water, shelter for breaks, and first aid facilities are all legal requirements — not optional luxuries.

→ Providing necessary information, training, instruction, and supervision. You cannot expect workers to perform tasks safely if they have not been properly trained and supervised. This duty applies especially to new workers, young workers, and workers performing unfamiliar tasks.

WORKERS
Workers also carry legal duties. A worker must:
→ Take reasonable care of their own health and safety.
→ Take reasonable care that their acts or omissions do not adversely affect the health and safety of others — including co-workers, visitors, members of the public passing by a site, and subcontractors.
→ Comply with any reasonable instruction given by the PCBU to allow the PCBU to comply with WHS laws.
→ Cooperate with any reasonable health and safety policy or procedure of the PCBU.

This means that a worker who ignores a personal protective equipment requirement, bypasses a safety guard, or fails to report a hazard is in breach of their own legal duty — regardless of any instructions from their employer.

OFFICERS
An Officer is a senior person within an organisation — a director, CEO, site manager, or any person who makes decisions affecting the whole or a substantial part of the business. Officers must exercise due diligence, which includes:
→ Acquiring and keeping up-to-date knowledge of work health and safety matters relevant to the operations.
→ Ensuring the PCBU has, and uses, appropriate resources and processes to eliminate or minimise risks.
→ Verifying that adequate processes exist to receive and consider information regarding incidents, hazards, and risks.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 3: REASONABLY PRACTICABLE — WHAT DOES IT MEAN?
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The phrase "so far as is reasonably practicable" is central to the WHS Act. It does not mean doing whatever is cheapest or most convenient. Reasonably practicable requires weighing up and balancing the following factors:

1. The likelihood of the hazard or risk eventuating — how probable is it that harm will occur?
2. The degree of harm that might result — a minor cut versus a fatality carry very different weights.
3. What the person concerned knows, or ought reasonably to know, about the hazard or risk and the ways of eliminating or minimising it.
4. The availability and suitability of ways to eliminate or minimise the risk.
5. After assessing points 1–4, the cost of eliminating or minimising the risk — and whether that cost is grossly disproportionate to the risk.

Critically, cost is only considered LAST. A PCBU cannot justify failing to implement an inexpensive safety measure simply because they chose not to prioritise it. The cost argument only becomes relevant when the risk is genuinely low AND the cost of the control is genuinely high.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 4: THE HIERARCHY OF RISK CONTROLS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The Hierarchy of Controls is mandated by the WHS Act and Regulations. It establishes a ranked order of effectiveness for controlling workplace risks. Controls higher on the hierarchy are more effective because they do not rely on human behaviour. The hierarchy must be applied in order — lower-level controls may only be used when higher-level controls are not reasonably practicable.

LEVEL 1 — ELIMINATION (Most Effective)
Physically remove the hazard altogether. This is the most effective control because the hazard no longer exists. Example: Instead of working at height to install roof sheeting, design the building so roof sheets are installed from the ground level using a ground-based system before erection. Or: Instead of hand-demolishing an asbestos-containing wall with workers inside the building, use remote-controlled demolition equipment.

LEVEL 2 — SUBSTITUTION
Replace the hazard with something less hazardous. Example: Replacing a toxic solvent-based paint with a water-based equivalent. Using a battery-powered nail gun instead of a pneumatic gun connected to a pressurised compressor in a confined area. Using plastic tilt-up panels instead of tilt-up concrete for partition walls.

LEVEL 2 — ISOLATION
Separate people from the hazard using physical barriers, distance, or enclosures. Example: Installing physical barricading around an excavation rather than relying on workers to stay away. Enclosing a noisy generator in a sound-attenuating shed away from the work area. Using remote controls to operate a crane rather than positioning the dogman directly under the suspended load.

LEVEL 2 — ENGINEERING CONTROLS
Design or modify equipment or the work environment to reduce risk. Example: Installing edge protection (guardrails, kickboards, and mid-rails) on all exposed working edges above 2 metres. Installing machine guards over moving parts of concrete saws and circular saws. Installing roll-over protective structures (ROPS) and falling-object protective structures (FOPS) on mobile plant cabs.

LEVEL 3 — ADMINISTRATIVE CONTROLS
Change the way work is performed to reduce exposure to hazards. Example: Rotating workers between noisy and quiet tasks to limit the duration of noise exposure. Implementing a work permit system that requires supervisor sign-off before any hot work, excavation, or confined space entry begins. Establishing a strictly enforced exclusion zone around a concrete pump truck during the entire pour.

LEVEL 3 — PERSONAL PROTECTIVE EQUIPMENT (PPE) — Least Effective
PPE is always the last line of defence — never the first. It does nothing to eliminate or reduce the hazard; it merely reduces the consequences if something goes wrong. PPE fails when it is not worn, not fitted correctly, damaged, or incompatible with the task. Example: A P2 respirator protects a worker exposed to silica dust, but only while it is correctly fitted to their face, undamaged, and changed at the correct intervals.

In practice, most risk control solutions use a combination of controls from multiple levels — which is known as using a hierarchy combination. For example, addressing silica dust when cutting concrete might involve: isolating the cutting area (isolation), using on-tool water suppression (engineering), limiting the duration of cutting tasks (administrative), and requiring P2 respirators (PPE) — all applied simultaneously.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 5: HIGH-RISK CONSTRUCTION WORK & SAFE WORK METHOD STATEMENTS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The WHS Regulations identify 18 specific categories of High-Risk Construction Work (HRCW) — activities that carry such inherently elevated risk that they demand additional controls beyond routine safe work procedures. When any HRCW is to be performed, a Safe Work Method Statement (SWMS) MUST be prepared before work begins.

The 18 HRCW categories include, among others:
→ Work involving a risk of a person falling more than 2 metres.
→ Work on telecommunications towers.
→ Work involving demolition of load-bearing elements of a structure.
→ Work involving the disturbance of asbestos.
→ Work on or near energised electrical installations.
→ Work in or near a confined space.
→ Work on or near pressurised gas pipelines or mains.
→ Work involving the use of explosives.
→ Work in an area with a temperature of 38°C or higher (in extreme heat).
→ Work involving tilt-up or precast concrete.
→ Work involving the erection or dismantling of formwork or falsework more than 2 metres high.
→ Work in an area where there is movement of powered mobile plant (such as excavators, dump trucks, or concrete agitators).

WHAT MUST A SWMS CONTAIN?
A SWMS is not just a compliance document — it is an active safety planning tool that must be site-specific and task-specific. It must:
→ Identify the specific HRCW activity covered.
→ Specify the hazards associated with the work.
→ Describe the risk control measures to be implemented, in accordance with the hierarchy.
→ Describe how those controls will be implemented.

WHO PREPARES IT?
The principal contractor (or the PCBU conducting the HRCW, if no principal contractor is appointed) is responsible for ensuring the SWMS is prepared. Workers who will perform the work must be directly consulted in the development of the SWMS and must be briefed on its contents before work begins. The SWMS must be readily available at the worksite while the HRCW is being performed.

WHEN IS A SWMS REVIEWED?
A SWMS must be reviewed and revised whenever: the HRCW to which it relates has not started and the SWMS may no longer reflect the way the work is to be carried out; or there is a change to the work, the work environment, or the controls.`
